<p>Mandan Hill, frequently called Crying Hill, has welcomed visitors and returning residents with its lettering for nearly a century. The landmark comprises three separate features: concrete letters spelling the city's name on the hill's northeastern face; the word "Mandan" spelled in trees on its southern face; and the hill itself, a sacred place of cultural importance to several Native nations.</p>

<h3>The Mandan sign</h3>

<p>During the 1920s, small towns across North Dakota and elsewhere placed their names on nearby hillsides. Signs remain at Bowman, Center, Kathryn, and New Salem. Before GPS and radio-navigation aids, the predecessor of the North Dakota Aeronautics Commission encouraged rural communities to mark their names on water towers and hillsides to help pilots identify their location during cross-country flights. Another explanation attributes the practice simply to civic pride.</p>

<p>The first Mandan sign was completed in 1929 by local Boy Scouts, who carried discarded paving blocks from the Sixth Avenue NW widening project to the hill. A nearby blacksmith shop provided whitewash. In 1932, <em>Mandan Pioneer</em> editor {% person "edwin-albert-tostevin-sr" %}Edwin Tostevin{% endperson %} proposed a larger, more substantial sign during a Mandan Lions Club meeting. After two years, civic leaders formally adopted the work as a community project.</p>

<p>The Mandan Boy Scouts led construction of a sign 300 feet long and 70 feet high on the southern face of Crying Hill in June 1934. Area businesses sponsored 47 truckloads of rocks collected from fields around the city. The Scouts used more than 12 gallons of white paint on the letters. The two middle characters—an uppercase N and D—were 85 feet high and represented North Dakota's official abbreviation. Local Boy and Girl Scouts maintained the sign for 30 years.</p>

