    <figcaption>The Mandan Hill sign in 1961</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>The sign was refurbished in June 1959. Missing stones were replaced, the letters were repainted, and railroad ties spelling "Trail West" were added to promote the new Custer drama opening at Fort Lincoln State Park that July. The Mandan Jaycees sponsored the work, with member {% person "larry-sullivan" %}Larry Sullivan{% endperson %} serving as project chairman.</p>

<p>In August 1963, Mandan Jaycees spent 12 hours and eight gallons of paint rehabilitating the sign. A second line of white-painted railroad timbers promoted the Custer Drama at Fort Lincoln State Park. In August 1974, 26 members of Mandan's TOPS Club 17, accompanied by several husbands, replaced missing rocks in both "MaNDan" and "MHS." The Chamber of Commerce provided white paint.</p>

<p>Construction of Interstate 94 north of the hill prompted the sign's relocation from the southern face to the eastern face in 1987, where it could be seen by approaching traffic. Large concrete panels replaced the loose stones to discourage vandalism. The marker has been described as North Dakota's largest sign.</p>

<p>As part of North Dakota's centennial celebration, Mandan planted a "Centennial Forest" in 1989 across the sign's original location on the southern face, with the trees also spelling "MANDAN." In September 2024, Doosan Bobcat employees cleared trees and brush around the concrete letters.</p>

