<p>A plan to scrape and repaint the concrete panels in spring 2025 grew into a more extensive proposal because of their condition. The Three Affiliated Tribes owns the land beneath the letters M, A, N, and D, while the City of Mandan owns the land beneath the final A and N. The city approached the Tribes about jointly sponsoring the project, and both agreed that the lettering needed attention.</p>

<p>Residents, city staff, and community leaders developed a plan around the theme "Honoring the past. Healing the future," suggested by the Four Directions group at Mandan High School. The preferred concept measured 20 feet high and 130 feet wide and included LED illumination. Its estimated cost was $1.2 million, to be raised through private sponsorships.</p>

<h3>Crying Hill</h3>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/landmarks/mandans-crying-hill/289-map-of-travels.jpg" alt="Map showing the route of early European explorers to the Mandan villages">
    <figcaption>Route map associated with early European travel to the Mandan villages</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>The sons of French explorer Pierre Gaultier de Varennes, sieur de La Vérendrye, were reportedly greeted in 1738 by Mandan chief Good Fur Blanket near the southern side of the hill. The hill's greater significance, however, comes from its importance to several Native nations. Mandan, Hidatsa, Lakota, Sioux, and Arikara traditions associate the place with sacred events and ceremonies.</p>

<p>As the highest point in the area, Crying Hill was sought as a place for prayer and solace. Pregnant women may have gone there to pray for healthy babies, while others undertook vision quests or sought guidance from departed loved ones. Native people of the region also used the area to mourn their dead.</p>

<p>Whether valued for its cultural significance or remembered as a childhood sledding hill, the place remains a historic Mandan landmark.</p>
